#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Warn when the disk is filling. `CHOICES.md disk-alert`.
#
# ONE `df`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. Every byte this repo worries about
# lives in the SAME btrfs pool as `/` -- Docker images in `@containerd`, volumes
# in `@dockervol`, snapshots in `@snapshots`, the package cache in `@pkg`. They
# are separate *mounts*, not separate filesystems. Checked on the box rather
# than assumed: `df -B1` reports identical used and size bytes for `/`, `/home`,
# `/var/lib/docker`, `/var/lib/containerd` and `/.snapshots`. So one number
# already sees all of them, and a per-subvolume meter is a second way of
# learning something this line has just been told.
#
# WHAT THE THREE-METER VERSION WAS FOR, AND WHY IT IS GONE. It watched the
# Docker qgroup *caps* (100 GiB and 50 GiB) and snapper's `SPACE_LIMIT` budget,
# warning as each was approached. Open question 26 deleted the caps -- a qgroup
# limit denied an fs-verity rollback and forced the whole filesystem read-only
# (`benchmarks/4.27`) -- and with them went the thing two of the three meters
# were measuring against. What is left to run out of is the filesystem, and that
# is what `df` reports.
#
# That deleted the read-only root helper and its `NOPASSWD` sudoers entry along
# with them, which is the part worth noticing: the simplification removed a
# custom root binary from the machine, not just lines from a file.
#
# Usage: disk-usage-alert        DISK_THRESHOLD=n overrides the 80% default.
set -Eeuo pipefail

pct=$(df --output=pcent / | tr -dc 0-9)
((${pct:-0} >= ${DISK_THRESHOLD:-80})) || exit 0

# `-t 0` so it stays until dismissed: a disk warning that times out while you
# are away is a warning that did not happen.
notify-send -u critical -a disk-monitor -i drive-harddisk -t 0 \
	"⚠️  Disk at ${pct}%" \
	"$(df -h / | awk 'NR==2 {print $3" used, "$4" free"}')
Usual suspects: lazydocker · snapper list · /var/cache/pacman/pkg"
